# The active theme: get_theme

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mtcars = Giggly.Datasets.mtcars()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`get_theme()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/get_theme.html), which covers
`theme_get()`, `set_theme()`, `theme_set()`, `update_theme()`, `theme_update()`,
`replace_theme()`, `theme_replace()` and `%+replace%` too, in the order the page gives them. Each
example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its
ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

ggplot2 keeps an active theme and applies it to every plot when the plot is drawn. `get_theme()`
returns it, `set_theme()` replaces it whole, and `update_theme()` and `replace_theme()` change
single elements of it; each of the three returns the theme it replaced, so that theme can be
restored. `theme_get()`, `theme_set()`, `theme_update()` and `theme_replace()` are other names for
the same four functions. Themes themselves combine with `+`, which changes only the properties a new
element sets, or with `%+replace%`, which replaces the element whole. Giggly keeps no global
settings, since every option is an argument, so there is no active theme to get or set: every plot
is drawn in ggplot2's default theme, `theme_gray()`, and there is no other theme and no way to
change one. A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns
an SVG document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines
`render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the cell with `Kino.Image`. The data in these
examples is `Giggly.Datasets.mtcars/0`, R's `mtcars`: 32 cars from the 1974 *Motor Trend* US
magazine, which the setup cell binds to `mtcars`, R's name for it.

## A plot in the default theme

```r
p <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(mpg, wt)) +
  geom_point()
p
```

Until the active theme is changed, ggplot2 draws in its default theme, `theme_gray()`, the one
Giggly draws every plot in.

```elixir
plot =
  mtcars
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :mpg, y: :wt})
  |> Plot.geom_point()

render.(plot)
```

## Setting the whole theme

```r
# Use set_theme() to completely override the current theme.
# update_theme() and replace_theme() are similar except they
# apply directly to the current/active theme.
# update_theme() modifies a particular element of the current theme.
# Here we have the old theme so we can later restore it.
# Note that the theme is applied when the plot is drawn, not
# when it is created.
old <- set_theme(theme_bw())
p
```

`set_theme()` makes `theme_bw()` the active theme and returns the theme it replaced, kept in
`old`. Giggly keeps no global settings, so there is no active theme to set, and every plot is
drawn in `theme_gray()`. Not in Giggly yet: `theme_bw()`.

## Updating one element

```r
set_theme(old)
update_theme(panel.grid.minor = element_line(colour = "red"))
p
```

`update_theme()` sets the colour of the active theme's minor grid lines and keeps the element's
other properties. Giggly has no active theme to update. Not in Giggly yet: changing the theme,
here to draw the minor grid lines in red.

## Replacing one element

```r
set_theme(old)
replace_theme(panel.grid.minor = element_line(colour = "red"))
p
```

`replace_theme()` replaces the element whole, so the properties the new element leaves unset are
unset in the theme too. Giggly has no active theme to change. Not in Giggly yet: changing the
theme, here to draw the minor grid lines in red.

## Restoring the theme

```r
set_theme(old)
p
```

`set_theme(old)` puts back the theme the first `set_theme()` replaced, and `p` is drawn in
`theme_gray()` again, as Giggly draws it.

```elixir
render.(plot)
```

## Modifying a theme object

`theme_grey()` is `theme_gray()` under its other spelling.

```r
# Modifying theme objects -----------------------------------------
# You can use + and %+replace% to modify a theme object.
# They differ in how they deal with missing arguments in
# the theme elements.

add_el <- theme_grey() +
  theme(text = element_text(family = "Times"))
add_el$text

rep_el <- theme_grey() %+replace%
  theme(text = element_text(family = "Times"))
rep_el$text
```

Each prints the `text` element of a theme made from `theme_grey()` with its font family set to
Times. Added with `+`, the element keeps the default theme's other text properties; replaced with
`%+replace%`, it has only the family, and its other properties are unset. Not in Giggly yet:
changing the theme, with `+` or `%+replace%`.
